WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.482
A sustainable CMMC program begins with governance.

2
00:00:03.482 --> 00:00:15.346
The organization needs accountable leaders, defined roles, approved policy, decision authority, issue escalation, supplier expectations, and a way to track changing contract obligations.

3
00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:19.846
Contract analysis and information discovery come before tool selection.

4
00:00:19.846 --> 00:00:32.717
Teams should understand applicable clauses, required CMMC status, FCI and CUI categories, data flows, recipients, locations, external services, and subcontractor relationships.

5
00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:35.620
A clear system boundary reduces uncertainty.

6
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:47.155
Inventories, diagrams, ownership, approved applications, security-protection assets, and external providers help the organization explain which environments are covered and why.

7
00:00:47.155 --> 00:00:51.675
Gap analysis compares current implementation with applicable requirements.

8
00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:58.310
Findings need accountable owners, realistic milestones, resources, risk visibility, and verification.

9
00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.900
A policy statement alone does not resolve a technical or operational gap.

10
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.025
Documentation should match reality.

11
00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:18.964
The System Security Plan, policies, procedures, inventories, diagrams, tickets, logs, training records, and supplier records should describe and demonstrate the way the environment actually operates.

12
00:01:18.964 --> 00:01:21.498
Supplier governance is part of the program.

13
00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:36.744
Organizations need processes to identify flow-down requirements, communicate handling expectations, review provider responsibilities, track status, and avoid sharing protected information before approved safeguards are in place.

14
00:01:36.744 --> 00:01:40.156
Continuous monitoring helps maintain the assessed condition.

15
00:01:40.156 --> 00:01:54.005
Access reviews, configuration checks, vulnerability management, incident response, change management, evidence review, training, and internal assessments can detect drift before it becomes a larger gap.

16
00:01:54.005 --> 00:01:55.841
Metrics should support decisions.

17
00:01:55.841 --> 00:02:09.470
Leaders need visibility into scope coverage, unresolved requirements, overdue remediation, supplier risk, training completion, evidence health, exceptions, incidents, and changes that could affect status.

18
00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:16.824
The most effective programs treat CMMC as part of normal contract performance and cybersecurity operations.

19
00:02:16.824 --> 00:02:24.108
Controls become routine work, evidence is produced naturally, and compliance supports protection rather than competing with it.

20
00:02:24.108 --> 00:02:25.325
CMMC 2.

21
00:02:25.325 --> 00:02:32.878
0 helps the Department of Defense verify safeguards for FCI and CUI across the Defense Industrial Base.

22
00:02:32.878 --> 00:02:40.312
Success depends on current official requirements, disciplined implementation, honest evidence, and continuous improvement.
